1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,247
The Cyberia wallet. The keys are made in
this browser and never leave it.

2
00:00:04,367 --> 00:00:08,246
It holds 1.4 CYBER — the coin this
network runs on.

3
00:00:08,366 --> 00:00:13,388
Trading is built in. There is no
separate exchange to go to.

4
00:00:13,508 --> 00:00:15,988
You pay in CYBER.

5
00:00:16,108 --> 00:00:20,137
You receive any token the chain has a
pool for.

6
00:00:20,257 --> 00:00:24,753
USDC — a dollar on Cyberia.

7
00:00:24,873 --> 00:00:28,018
Half a coin.

8
00:00:28,138 --> 00:00:30,675
Every pool on the chain is priced — both
versions of the exchange — and the best
route wins.

9
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:34,959
0.5 CYBER buys 0.033572 USDC. Price
impact: 0.00%.

10
00:00:35,079 --> 00:00:40,168
The route, the slippage and the floor
are all on the screen before anything is
signed.

11
00:00:40,288 --> 00:00:44,367
This trade goes WCYBER → USDT → USDC —
two pools, one transaction.

12
00:00:44,487 --> 00:00:48,569
The direct pool was quoted too. It pays
13.65% less.

13
00:00:48,689 --> 00:00:57,630
The floor is written into the
transaction: at least 0.033404 USDC, or
the swap reverts and nothing is spent.

14
00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:02,230
The whole trade in one sentence, in
plain language.

15
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:08,568
Hold to sign — a tap is something a
thumb does by accident.

16
00:01:08,688 --> 00:01:13,856
Signed on the device, then broadcast to
Cyberia.

17
00:01:13,976 --> 00:01:23,816
Traded. The CYBER is now USDC.

18
00:01:23,936 --> 00:01:28,093
And it is in the same wallet — one
transaction, no account anywhere.
